in this lesson we're going to talk about  multiplying matrices so what is a matrix  how do you multiply matrices we're going  to get into all that in this lesson  first of all what a matrix is it's a way  of arranging data or organizing data  instead of like a a list you're making  it more like into a table or a chart  okay it's called a matrix and all these  quantities are called elements but the  main thing you want to know about a  matrix are  you know how many rows does it have and  how many columns does it have so first  of all the rows they go this way  horizontally and the columns they go  vertically like this so this matrix here  has one two rows  and it has one two three columns columns  like the roman columns columns on a  building so it's a two by three matrix  so you always want to do rows first and  column second so for this matrix it has  one two three rows  and one column right so three by one and  this one has one two rows and two  columns this is actually called a square  matrix because it's square squared has  the same number of rows as columns but  when you multiply matrices you always  want to multiply  the matrices in such a way that see this  is rows by columns for the first matrix  rows by columns for the second matrix  you want to make sure that these inner  dimensions that they match that they're  the same otherwise the matrices are  incompatible you can't multiply them  together you're not going to it's not  going to work just not possible and then  what you end up with is you end up with  these two quantities here at the end the  number of rows here and the number of  columns here so for example if you have  a three by two matrix times a two by one  matrix this matrix multiplication is  possible these inner dimensions match  and you're going to end up with a three  by one matrix so that's going to be the  resultant size of your matrix it's going  to have three rows  okay like this  and one column so let's go through an  example see if you can pause the video  and do this one on your own we'll go  through it together so here it says what  is a times b so here's our matrix a  here's our matrix b first thing we want  to do is we want to make sure that  they're compatible so we have a two by  three  and a three by one so the inner  dimensions match we're going to end up  with a two by one two rows  okay and one column so this is two rows  this way and one column vertically  okay so the next thing we're going to do  is we're going to multiply the row  times the columns we're going to take  this first row times the first column so  if we do that we get  1 times 1 which is 1  plus 3 times 2 which is  6 plus 2 times 6 which is 12. if we add  those together we get 19.  now if we take the  second row times the second column and  by the way when you do this sometimes  it's helpful to  go ahead and work backwards you say well  what position is this in it's in the  second row  first column so you always do rows by  column so second row first column that  tells you to take the second row times  the first column and what i think about  doing is i think about taking this row  and rotating it like this so that the  first element in the row matches up with  the first element the column second  element in the row second with the  column third or third so it's like this  you're rotating and the ones that match  up you're multiplying and then you add  them together so in this case zero times  one is  zero plus negative one times two is  negative 2 plus 5 times 6 is 30 and if  you add those together you can see  you're getting 28  so this is our resultant matrix right  here now you can see if we tried to  multiply like matrix b and c we would  have an issue because these inner  dimensions they don't match see one and  two so that's just not going to be  possible so that's how you do matrix  multiplication i'll see you in the next  lesson
 

